    "teacher_markdown": "### What’s happening in this level\r\nThe page already has JavaScript written for all features.\r\n\r\nStudents are using AI to:\r\n- connect a button or input to that code by have AI tutor add an event listener\r\n\r\nThey are NOT writing any code. They are just prompting the AI to make one interaction work. They do not need to know what an event listener is ... they will learn that next lesson. They only need to know how to prompt the AI Tutor to describe which button or input to get working.\r\n\r\n### Goal for this level\r\nStudents experience how a webpage becomes interactive by making one part of the page respond to an action.\r\n\r\nThrough this, they begin to understand that:\r\n- parts of a webpage can change\r\n- those changes happen in the DOM\r\n- this is what allows a webpage to be interactive\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### What to look for\r\nStudents should:\r\n- choose ONE button or input\r\n- describe what they want to happen\r\n- get the interaction working\r\n\r\nSuccess = something on the page changes when the user acts (clicks or types)\r\n\r\nKeep focus on the interaction, not the code.\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Common issues\r\n- Students try to fix everything instead of one interaction\r\n- Prompts are too vague (“make it work”)\r\n- Students don’t test after the AI Tutor adds code\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n"
  },
